What it does
------------
Renders the standard LaTeX math delimiters — inline `\(…\)` and display
`\[…\]` — alongside Obsidian's native `$…$` / `$$…$$`, without ever rewriting
the note on disk.

Why it is imported directly instead of through a require bridge
---------------------------------------------------------------
The whole module is two files, and it compiles with ZERO errors under the
host's much stricter tsconfig (`strict`, `noUnusedLocals`,
`noUnusedParameters` — the upstream one only sets `noImplicitAny` and
`strictNullChecks`). That is the "source-direct" verdict from the merge
playbook, so it is a plain `import` in src/main.ts rather than a `require`
bridge.

Changes relative to upstream
----------------------------
Kept whole: parser.ts, the Live Preview StateField, the widget behaviour,
the reading-view DOM surgery (Range + deleteContents + insertNode), the
`data-latex-delimiter-renderer-section` re-entry guard.

1. Reading View positioning (`src/main.ts`).

   Upstream located a formula with `rendered.text.indexOf(visibleSource)` —
   a plain search from offset 0. Right almost always, wrong twice, and both
   failures are invisible to upstream's suite because it never runs a real
   markdown parser:

     a. A literal `(…)` appearing EARLIER in the section is found first and
        gets turned into math. Example: `(c) 是记号，公式是 \(c\)` renders
        the ordinary `(c)` as a formula.

     b. A body markdown rewrote into elements is never found at all, so the
        formula silently stays raw. `\(a_1 + b_2\)` reaches the DOM as
        `(a<em>1 + b</em>2)` — the underscores are gone. LaTeX subscripts
        make this the COMMON case, not a corner one.

   Replaced with `locateVisible()`, three bounded tries:

     1. exact match at/after the predicted position — nearly every formula;
     2. the same, backed up by DRIFT_WINDOW (120) — for emphasis lost in the
        prose in front of the formula, which pushes it EARLIER than predicted;
     3. a normalised match (`stripEmphasis`) for emphasis inside the body.

   The predicted position comes from `stripMarkdownEscapes` applied to the
   section source in front of the match, and the needle is
   `visibleForm()` — the shape the formula has AFTER markdown ate the
   backslashes (`\(` is a legal CommonMark escape, since `(` is ASCII
   punctuation). Also slices `getSectionInfo().text` by
   `lineStart`/`lineEnd`; upstream searched whole-document text, which makes
   offsets unusable for positioning.

   A miss degrades to "not rendered", never to "the wrong text became math".
   `locateVisible` returns `to` separately because a normalised match can be
   SHORTER than the needle.

2. An API published on `app` (key `latex-delimiter-renderer`).

   Reading View and Live Preview are covered by the two registrations, but
   the Logseq module renders several surfaces from markdown it owns ITSELF:
   static blocks, block embeds, native embeds, flashcards, a code block's
   prefix, and the out-of-vault file view. Those call
   `MarkdownRenderer.render` into a plain container, where `getSectionInfo`
   returns null (it needs the reading view's section map), so the post
   processor cannot reach them. They convert the source instead, via
   `convertForObsidianRender`.

   Publishing rather than having Logseq import the module keeps the
   dependency one-way, and makes the host's on/off switch darken every
   surface at once — a static import would leave those six rendering math
   while Live Preview and Reading View had stopped.

3. `stripMarkdownEscapes`, `stripEmphasis`, `sectionTextOf`, `visibleForm`
   and `locateVisible` are exported so they can be unit-tested
   (`tests/readingMatch.test.ts`). Nothing else was made public.

4. Upstream's tests were `node:test` + tsx; re-expressed in vitest to match
   the host (`tests/parser.test.ts`). The assertions are unchanged.

5. styles.css is merged into the host's styles.css under
   `/* ==================== LaTeX Delimiter Renderer (modules/latex-delimiter-renderer) ==================== */`.
   The host stylesheet is hand-maintained and must be merged into, never
   overwritten.

Surfaces covered
----------------
  Live Preview (native editor)  registerEditorExtension
  Reading View (native)         registerMarkdownPostProcessor
  Logseq block editor           same extension — the outline pulls in
                                workspace.editorExtensions
  Logseq static blocks          convertForObsidianRender (renderTree)
  Block embeds                  convertForObsidianRender (links)
  Native embeds                 convertForObsidianRender (nativeEmbed)
  Flashcards                    convertForObsidianRender (flashcardPanel)
  Code-block prefix             convertForObsidianRender (BlockEditorView)
  Out-of-vault files            convertForObsidianRender (ExternalFileView)

